---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import Img from '../../components/Img.astro';
import Media from '../../components/Media.astro';
import Video from '../../components/Video.astro';
---
<CaseLayout
  slug="lyft"
  eyebrow="Course case competition · Management 1000"
  title="Lyft Explore"
  summary="A feature concept pitching Lyft on turning every bike ride into a city adventure, driving engagement, loyalty, and a new revenue stream built entirely on existing infrastructure."
  hero="/images/lyft/hero.webp"
  heroAlt="Lyft Explore logo with a pink gem and two leaderboard screens"
  snapshot={[
    ['My role', 'Headed ideation, slide design, and every mockup'],
    ['Context', 'Class case competition'],
    ['Focus', 'Micromobility growth'],
    ['Modeling', 'Excel + Google Sheets'],
  ]}
>
  <section id="overview" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Overview">Project overview</h2>
    <p>
      This case competition in my Management 1000 course challenged us to dig deep into Lyft’s business model and find
      real room to grow.
    </p>
    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><b>2</b><span>rides per bike, per day. Nowhere near enough to cover fixed infrastructure costs.</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>~90%</b><span>of Lyft’s total revenue came from rideshare, leaving bikes and scooters as a costly side project.</span></div>
    </div>
    <p>
      Rather than blending in with competitors like Lime and Bird, who offer the same bikes, the same maps, and the same
      pricing, I focused on a differentiator that fits Lyft’s core values: <strong>community</strong> and
      <strong>getting people out there exploring</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The solution gamifies urban exploration through a GemPoints rewards system, turning every bike ride into an
      adventure while building on infrastructure Lyft already owns.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="research" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Research">1. Deep research + discovery</h2>
    <p>
      The process followed a deliberate sequence: absorb everything about Lyft, find where the real opportunity was,
      then design something that felt native to who Lyft already is.
    </p>
    <p>
      I read Lyft’s 10-K filings, micromobility market data, Gen Z behavioral research, and competitor positioning,
      looking for growth that competitors weren’t addressing.
    </p>
    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><b>$4.5B → $9.61B</b><span>global micromobility market, 2025 to 2030. No single company owns the space.</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>65%</b><span>of Pokémon GO’s 106M monthly users are 18 to 34, Lyft’s core demographic.</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>+150%</b><span>boost in user engagement from gamification, alongside a 25.3% lift in sales.</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="wedge" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Inspiration">2. Finding the wedge</h2>
    <p>
      The goal was not to copy competitors. It was to find a differentiator that matched Lyft’s existing brand identity:
      community, movement, and exploration. I pulled references for tone and interaction patterns from four places.
    </p>
    <div class="features">
      <div class="feature"><h3>Location play</h3><p>Pokémon GO turns real places into reasons to go outside.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><h3>Friendly competition</h3><p>Strava and Nike Run Club leaderboards keep people coming back.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><h3>Branded map moments</h3><p>Lyft Media already swaps vehicle icons during movie premieres.</p></div>
    </div>
    <Media src="/images/lyft/moodboard.webp" alt="Mood board: Pokémon GO map, Strava leaderboard, Lyft Media event icons, Nike Run Club leaderboard, and a hidden gem video thumbnail" ratio="wide" caption="Mood board of similar platforms: Pokémon GO, Strava, Snap Map, Nike Run Club, and Lyft Media’s event mode." />
  </section>

  <section id="solution" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Solution">3. Proposing Lyft Explore</h2>
    <p>
      We landed on <strong>Lyft Explore</strong>, a UI/UX layer built directly into the existing Lyft app map.
      Gem Pinpoints, sponsored and community hot spots, appear on the map using the same icon mechanic Lyft Media
      already uses for event sponsorships.
    </p>
    <Media src="/images/lyft/evolution.webp" alt="Three phones: today's Lyft bikes map, the gem concept map, and the final dark map with a sponsored gem" ratio="wide" caption="From today’s map, to the gem concept, to the final dark-mode map with a sponsored location." />
    <div class="features">
      <div class="feature"><h3>Gem Pinpoints</h3><p>Sponsored and community hot spots on the map, pointing riders toward places worth seeing.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><h3>Gem Points</h3><p>An optional points layer earned per ride, built to plug into Lyft’s existing rewards programs.</p></div>
      <div class="feature"><h3>Leaderboard</h3><p>A regional leaderboard where riders compete for Lyft credit. Sponsors can add their own prizes.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p>
      Lyft already runs its own rewards: a points-based Lyft Rewards program since 2018, and Lyft Cash Rewards,
      launched in late 2025. The points layer would integrate with that infrastructure instead of running separately.
    </p>
    <Media src="/images/lyft/payout.webp" alt="Brand-sponsored and Lyft leaderboards beside a base reward payout: 1st $50, 2nd $30, 3rd $15 in Lyft credit" ratio="wide" caption="Base reward payout in Lyft credit: 1st $50, 2nd $30, 3rd $15. A sponsoring brand can add a prize on top." />
  </section>

  <section id="design" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Final design">The final screens</h2>
    <Media src="/images/lyft/final-screens.webp" alt="Final screens: a brand-sponsored leaderboard, the Lyft leaderboard, and the map with a 300-point gem" ratio="wide" />
    <p>It also lives beyond the phone, on the dock kiosk and the bike’s handlebar screen.</p>
    <div class="row row-2">
      <Media src="/images/lyft/kiosk-map.webp" alt="Bike dock kiosk showing a sponsored leaderboard next to the app map" ratio="wide" />
      <Media src="/images/lyft/handlebar.webp" alt="Lyft bike handlebar screen showing the gem map" ratio="wide" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="campaign" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Campaign">Campaign: on the street and in the feed</h2>
    <p class="quote">“Every ride is a hidden gem.”</p>
    <p>Get rewarded for exploring. The launch idea pairs out-of-home screens near docks with creator-style social video.</p>
    <div class="row row-2">
      <Media src="/images/lyft/campaign.webp" alt="Campaign key visual: Every ride is a hidden gem, with a Lyft scooter" ratio="wide" />
      <Media src="/images/lyft/billboard.webp" alt="Digital billboard mockup of the campaign outside a building" ratio="wide" />
    </div>
    <div class="campaign-vids">
      <Video name="lyft-ride-concept" label="Concept video: riders exploring the city while a gem floats overhead and the leaderboard updates" caption="How we imagine it: every ride earns points toward the regional leaderboard." />
      <Video name="lyft-explore-with-me" frame="bare" label="Creator-style social video: Come explore with me, Lyft Explore edition" caption="Social concept: “Come explore with me, Lyft Explore edition.”" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="why" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Why it matters">Why it matters</h2>
    <p>
      Lyft’s differentiation rests on connection and community rather than price, since Uber already competes hard on
      cost. Gem Pinpoints extend that idea to micromobility: they act as a guide, pointing riders toward real places
      worth visiting, so a ride becomes a way to actually explore the city.
    </p>
    <p>
      A fair critique of gamifying a mobility app is that it could keep people glued to their phones. But with Gem
      Pinpoints, the app is only the guide, not the destination. Points are earned by riding a bike or scooter to a
      real location, so the app points people outward instead of pulling them further in.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="results" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Results">Projected results</h2>
    <p>Projections were built in Excel and Google Sheets using Divvy bike usage data and Lyft’s segment revenue as a share of total revenue.</p>
    <div class="stats solid">
      <div class="stat"><b>720% ROI</b><span>Break-even within the first year</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>87.7% margin</b><span>Existing infrastructure makes it possible</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>$213M → $317M</b><span>Projected 5-year profit</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="reflection" class="case-section">
    <h2 data-short="Reflection">Reflection</h2>
    <p>
      What I found most meaningful was the room to bring entrepreneurial thinking into an existing brand: building an
      innovative proposal from the ground up while keeping revenue at the center of every decision. It’s a different
      kind of challenge than executing inside an existing brand system, and it’s part of why I enjoy work that sits at
      the intersection of ideas and business case.
    </p>
    <p>
      This project relied on secondary research and comparisons to other products, not conversations with actual Lyft
      riders. That’s a limitation worth naming, and one I keep in mind for how this project fits alongside the rest of my work.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseLayout>

<style>
  .campaign-vids { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.9fr 1fr; gap: 20px; align-items: start; margin-top: 24px; }
  @media (max-width: 760px) { .campaign-vids { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
